speechtext:
Whether it was on a Thursday or a Friday is not the point;
I do not think that has any bearing on what we are discussing now. I say that hitherto it has been considered that an interim Supply Bill did not affect in any way the rights of members of the House with regard to the balance of the Estimates. It was never considered that because an interim Supply Bill had been passed the Estimates of all departments had been taken up. What we want to know is whether my right hon. friend and the Government will consider that when this

Bill passes they have the right to take up any Estimates on Thursdays and Fridays that ha.ve not been taken up previously in the present session, such as the Estimates of the Labour Department, the Interior Department, the Militia Department, and many others. As my hon. friend f!rom Pontiac has said, this may have some bearing on the discussion on the present Bill.

The present law, paragraph V, of sub-section (1) of section 9 of the Post Office Act, provides that the Postmaster General may:
(v) Establish a system providing for indemnity for losses of registered mailable matter, such indemnity in no case to exceed twenty-five dollars for any one registered piece, or the actual value thereof if less than twenty-five dollars, and fix an insurance fee or a scale of insurance fees to be prepaid in respect of such mailable matter, and, from time to time, make such regulations as he deems necessary for carrying out such system;
The change proposed by the amendment is to establish a system under which the maximum value of insurable mail matter shall be $100 in any one case, and to fix a scale of insurance fees, payable in respect of postage, up to $100. The actual value of the article, or the parcel, may be stated by the person who sends the mail matter and on that value the fee will be fixed.

The Honourable William' Stevens Fielding, a member of His Majesty's Honourable Privy Council for Canada, a Member of the Parliament of Canada, Minister of Finance and Receiver General of Canada;
The Honourable Louis-Philippe Brodeur, a Member of His Majesty's Honourable Privy Council for Canada, a Member of the Parliament of Canada, Minister of Marine and Fisheries of Canada ;
And the President of the French Republic;
Mr. Stephen Pichon, Senator, Minister of Foreign Affairs;
Mr. Joseph Caillaux, Deputy, Minister of Finance;
Mr. Gaston Doumergue, Deputy, Minister of Commerce and Industry;
Mr. Joseph Ruau, Deputy, Minister of Agriculture ;
Who, after communicating to each other their respective full powers, found in good and due form, have agreed upon the following articles:-
There was the usual form of a treaty. There His Majesty's existence was recognized. Nothing of the kind is found in this agreement. I think my hon. friend

who is not very anxious to have His Majesty take any part in this matter should be gratified; he is having his way. I stand for the old form. I want treaties to be negotiated by Canadians for Canadian business, but as ministers appointed as plenipotentiaries for the purpose under the sign manual of His Majesty the King of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland and of the British Dominions beyond the Seas, Emperior of India.
